welcome back now employers are being  urged to review their bereavement leave  policy as part of this year's pregnancy  and infant loss remembrance day it comes  after a campaign aimed at getting bosses  to commit to supporting their staff  through the distress of miscarriage one  company that's announcing a new  pregnancy loss policy today is flow  health a period and ovulation tracker  app for joining me now to talk about  this is anne roberts she's chief people  officer at flow and claudia pasted she's  the company's medical advisor uh welcome  to you both um claudia let me start with  uh with you i mean how widespread is  pregnancy loss  yeah it's incredibly widespread it's  actually much more common pregnancy loss  is far more common than we realize  and if we speak about miscarriage for  example for which the definition varies  across the world but in the uk it's the  loss of a pregnancy during the first 23  weeks  one in eight known pregnancies end in  miscarriage  and the reality beyond this number is we  often don't know that our friend or our  colleague is going through this  experience and not everybody finds it  easy to talk about it either  so we know that no matter the nature of  the loss the moment when it happens its  effects can be profound and long-lasting  and it affects everybody differently too  so 30 to 50 percent of people who have  miscarried might experience anxiety 10  to 15 percent depression and also one in  six women who have a miscarriage or  ectopic pregnancy experience long-term  post-traumatic stress so it's important  to support people at the time of their  loss but also beyond that after and  that's what we hope to achieve with that  with our pregnancy loss policy  so ann what are you going to be changing  what's going to be new about what you do  um so we're going to be introducing um a  pregnancy loss policy that includes  anybody who is going through the  experience regardless of their service  whether in the world what stage of  pregnancy they're in  whether they're the expecting mother or  the partner  to be able to take two weeks off no  questions asked self-certified leave  fully paid um also including more  support in return to work and more  resources for both employees going  through the experience and  their managers and their colleagues on  on how to support um and create an  empathetic environment in the workplace  for this  and will that policy be extended to  employees who've gone through an  abortion  or is it just confined to those who've  had a miscarriage  yes it includes anybody who is going  through abortion um as well as if it  happens to a baby surrogate mother if  you're a surrogate parent  claudia you mentioned just now that this  is not something that is is widely  talked about why do you think that is  because i mean i imagine everyone  watching this program i certainly do  know someone who's been through a  miscarriage  yeah that's right i mean there is i  think lots of us are aware of this idea  that you you can't you feel like you  can't talk about your pregnancy until  you've had your scan at 12 weeks and we  know that most miscarriages actually  happen before 12 weeks so there seems to  be sort of this idea that you don't talk  about and therefore when a miscarriage  happens in that period of time you you  have nobody to turn to or to speak to  about it so i think that's that's  possibly something that's contributing  to it it's astonishing that there are as  many miscarriages in the world as there  are these days given the advances that  we've seen in medical technology  yes but there are certain things like  this that that  we can't stop and unfortunately can't  always prevent about 70 percent of  miscarriages actually happen because of  chromosomal abnormalities and these  aren't really things that we know how to  prevent or to stop unfortunately  so and why is it do are there many other  companies out there that you're aware of  that have a pregnancy loss policy like  the one you've introduced  uh  yes we're not complete pioneers in the  field um we're  um trailblazing um  along with uh four other companies and  that i know of in the uk who have  introduced this  um and i'm  certain that with the pandemic and the  increased focus on  mental health well-being  creating supportive workplaces friend  please you know  long gone are the days of the 2000s  where  the employer value proposition was about  free booze and bouncy castles in office  um the conversation in the industry for  employers let's move on to how do you  create an inclusive workplace that  recognizes that people have a life  outside of their work  and making a meaningful impact in that  support um so i'm seeing the trend  continuing for for businesses  well hopefully the fact that you've come  on today to talk about this might  encourage a few more businesses to to  follow your example uh i mean tell me  about the business how's the business  going right now  um so um flow is the world's most  popular um health and witness fitness  app um we have over 43 million active  million  active users 1.6 million of them are in  the uk alone  the business is growing incredibly  strongly  and that's off the back of their  heightened awareness in society  about  about menstrual health about supporting  women from  uh early stages of motherhood or from  first getting administration all the way  to menopause um so the focus on on  reproductive health and female health is  is incredibly  um growing field and off the back of  that where we're leading the empowerment  of women around the world so business is  doing very well  good to hear well congratulations on a  very worthy initiative and claudia good  to talk to you both this morning thank  you
 

